Two layers of protection were added:

Application-level guard — before any insert, cmd_fetch queries for an existing snapshot matching today's UTC date and period_days. If one is found it exits cleanly with a descriptive message telling you exactly which snapshot already covers today.

Database-level constraint — a UNIQUE (fetch_date, period_days) constraint on the snapshots table acts as a hard backstop. Even if somehow the app-level check were bypassed, the DB will reject the duplicate insert.

A new fetch_date column (YYYY-MM-DD) was added to make the uniqueness check efficient and unambiguous — it's derived from fetched_at and backfilled for any existing rows on first run.

The new --force flag lets you deliberately replace today's snapshot when you need it:

bash
python brew_analytics.py fetch           # blocked if today already has a snapshot
python brew_analytics.py fetch --force   # replaces today's snapshot cleanly
